00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.810
Let's sign in.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.120
All you need is your Entra ID.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.260
After login, you land in a page
that shows all your projects.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.290
Inside project, you can have
one or more applications

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.020
and other shared
resources like sandbox,

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.030
models, network policies.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.670
Project admins can add
developers to the projects

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.950
and give them permissions
depending on their role

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.240
and required activity.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.220
Let's talk about sandbox.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.580
It's an isolated environment
to run GitHub Copilot agents

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.460
with native file, shell,
and web operations

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.410
with isolated execution.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.360
These agents have
native capabilities

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.810
to write code dynamically
and support skills and MCPs

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.760
for their reasoning and actions.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.300
The sandbox is configured at
the project with repositories,

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.040
skills, and other context.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:41.370
Inside a project, you have
one or more applications.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.740
Apps are where developers build.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.250
Each app holds its workflows,
connections, parameters,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.640
and analytics dashboard, and
its own permission model,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.190
reader or contributor,
so you can collaborate

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.470
without exposing the
rest of the project.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.310
Let's go ahead and create
workflow inside the application.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.290
So I'll first create my workflow

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.660
by describing it in
natural language.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.710
The agent will analyze this
request and scaffold a workflow.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.800
The generated workflow still
needs its connections updated,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.370
but a lot of the work is already
taken care of by Copilot,

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:15.360
picking the connectors

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.790
and configuring many
of the parameters.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.270
From here, I can iteratively
build and test the workflow

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.130
with Copilot's assistance.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.400
When asked to write code,
Copilot added the action

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.180
and wrote JavaScript code with
appropriate exception handling.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.570
Developers can make updates
at the workflow scope or go

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.850
to the action scope
and have Copilot make

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.620
action-specific updates.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.350
We use a default LLM
that's hosted for you,

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.150
but you can use your
GitHub account to bring

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.610
in additional models,
including both first-party

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.050
and third-party models.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.350
Here's a workflow I've already
built for invoice processing.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.020
Real-world problems most of
the time need to be solved

00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:58.830
with a combination

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:01.650
of deterministic steps
and AI reasoning.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.560
That's the power of
workflow automation here.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.320
In this workflow,
ServiceNow ticket creation

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.740
and Salesforce update
are deterministic steps.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:14.130
The invoice agent is provided
a system prompt describing

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.140
its goals.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:19.250
The agent here is built on the
GitHub Copilot SDK and runs

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.670
on a sandbox, the isolated
compute per workflow configured

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:23.790
at project.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.860
The business context
like invoice,

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.510
and skills for validation

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.360
and notification are
provided to the agent here.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:34.280
You can even provide GitHub
or Azure DevOps repositories.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.950
After the invoice validation
process completes,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.470
we again have a set of
deterministic steps

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.780
to update Salesforce
and close the ticket

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.700
if the invoice was approved.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.680
Alongside the visual workflow,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.310
the code view shows an editable
view into the same workflow.

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:51.170
You can make updates
in code view

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.630
and see the workflow
update in real time.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.110
Copilot assistant can
be used here, too,

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.720
to write JavaScript
expressions or code.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.340
Being able to scope Copilot

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.220
to a specific action
lets you make targeted,

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:12.930
action-specific updates.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.120
Here, the agent is
responding with code

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.430
in the scope of
JavaScript action.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.350
I can have multi-turn
conversations

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.080
with agent to iterate over code.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:25.110
When an invoice is received,
the workflow triggers

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:27.660
and the run history
shows full traceability

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.270
and auditability
into every step.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.300
With real-time streaming
capabilities, you can view

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.460
into each step while
it's happening.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:39.000
For an agent node, you can
visually see, in real time,

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.700
the tools it's using
to accomplish tasks.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:45.750
Every step of the workflow is
logged with inputs and outputs,

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.270
execution time, and status.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:49.860
Agent outcomes are logged,

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:52.860
including the token
utilization at each step.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.200
That's Logic Apps Automation.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.800
Low barrier to entry with
same enterprise rigor.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.800
Preview is live today,
and we can't wait

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.850
to see you build with it.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:07.860
DIVYA SWARNKAR: So now that
we have watched the demo,

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.720
let's take a quick
look under the hood

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.440
so that we can understand
the underlying model better.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.870
The topmost construct
is a project.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.360
This is something where
an admin operates.

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.580
You can think of projects
as environments,

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:23.610
and these can be scoped by
your development environments

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:25.500
or by your business domains.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.560
It's up to you how you want
to think about organization

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.510
of your business workflows.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.040
Projects is how your platform

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.950
and infrastructure
team controls access,

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:38.100
networking policies,
and shared resources.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:41.070
Inside a project, developers
create applications,

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:44.070
one or multiple,
however many they need.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.250
And then at the project level,
you have shared resources

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.630
that the platform team manages,
and every app can consume.

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.630
These can include things like
knowledge for grounding,

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.960
sandboxes for your
isolated agent compute,

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.570
policies for governance
and guardrails,

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:04.290
and ability to include
and exclude connectors.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:09.120
Each app has its own workflows,
connections, files, and skills.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.150
This is what builders
work in day-to-day.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:14.190
They don't need Azure
access anymore,

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:16.710
and so this offers
a clear separation

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.750
where admins govern
the platform,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.990
and developers build on the top.

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:23.880
The platform offers two layers

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.610
of governance mapped
to two roles.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.320
Layer one is for project.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:32.190
This is an Azure resource
governed by ARM RBAC.

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:36.030
The project admin owns it,
and they manage members,

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:40.620
permissions, policies, quotas,
knowledge, sandboxes, models,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.690
scoped at the project level.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.990
Layer two is where
developers or builder lives.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:48.810
They build apps, which
includes workflows,

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:51.030
connections, files, and skills.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:53.220
Here, no Azure
access is required;

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.320
no Azure permissions are needed.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.740
They consume project-scoped
resources within the policies

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:01.680
that the admin set for them.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.720
So as you can clearly see,

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.600
one team governs while
another team builds,

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:10.350
and both of them inside
the same secure boundary.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:14.490
Building a RAG pipeline
today is quite complex.

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:16.230
There are multiple
steps involved,

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:20.160
including preparing the vector
store and data to be ingested,

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.950
and then building the
pipeline to parse, chunk,

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.980
and tokenize the documents.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:29.700
We have collapsed this process
into few simple steps.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:31.320
You upload the files.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:34.140
Logic Apps automatically
ingests, chunks,

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.240
and vectorizes the content.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.070
There is no pipeline
to configure.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.860
There is no infrastructure
to maintain by you.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.490
Retrieval is also a
native workflow step.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.760
For anyone building grounded,
personalized agents,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.190
this dramatically
lowers the barrier.

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:52.830
Let me show you how this works.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:55.260
SPEAKER 1: Here we are in a
Logic App where we'll set

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.920
up a knowledge base and then use
it from an agent in a workflow.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.260
The knowledge base is
scoped at the app level,

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.020
so you can share it across
workflows in the same app.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.780
Before you start, there's
a one-time setup.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:10.380
This capability is built on
Cosmos DB as the vector store

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.870
and uses Azure
OpenAI for models.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.520
That's what we configure here.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.880
You connect the knowledge
base to a Cosmos DB instance,

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.520
then provide model for
embedding generation

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.470
and another for
chat completions.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.360
Here's an app where I've
already completed that setup.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.450
Once configured, you can add
files to your knowledge base

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:35.400
and organize them into groups.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:38.070
The groups are the unit of
reference from an agent,

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.110
so you can logically
group your files based

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.510
on the scenario and
desired outcome.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:45.630
When you upload a file, you
choose the group it belongs to

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:48.630
and add metadata like
name and description.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:52.230
Since each file goes through
tokenization, chunking,

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.990
and embedding, we show
the status so you know

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.130
when it's ready to use.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.020
Here's a workflow with an agent

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:12.300
that classifies
incoming documents,

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:14.400
and if it's a contract,
then validates

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:17.850
against the organization's
approval rules and policies.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.730
Naturally, it needs to be
grounded in those rules.

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.790
In the agent, you'll see
a "Knowledge" section.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:26.190
The connection is
already set up.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:28.380
Here, you select the specific
knowledge group you want

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.030
to make available to the agent,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.090
and you can add files
directly from this view.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:36.300
Below that are the instructions
guiding the agent's behavior.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.570
Behind the scenes, the agent
performs semantic search

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.850
and retrieval against your
knowledge group based

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:42.870
on the query.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.330
What would normally take
significant effort to build

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:46.650
as a custom ingestion

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:50.260
and retrieval pipeline is
fully handled for you.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.960
We have uploaded a document
classification guide

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:58.630
and contract processing
guide for this scenario.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:07.260
Let's run the workflow
with a sample contract.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:10.620
In the run history, you'll see
the agents first classified the

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:13.650
document and identified
as a contract.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.530
It then went through the
contract validation steps.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.260
All the agent processing
and responses are grounded

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:22.140
in the classification
and validation document,

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.000
with references to
knowledge surfaced

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.790
in both the agent chat
and the run history.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.700
The agent node also shows
the specific knowledge group

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.100
that was used, along
with token utilization,

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.810
so you can track usage.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.420
And that's knowledge
in Logic Apps.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.670
Now available in public preview.

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:41.730
Give it a try and bring your
organization's documents

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:47.010
into your agents without writing
a single line of pipeline code.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.210
DIVYA SWARNKAR:
Next, I want to talk

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.670
about how we are bringing
the capabilities

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:54.750
of two Azure services into
a cohesive experience.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.390
On the left, we have
Azure AI Foundry.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:59.760
This is the Microsoft's
AI platform

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.160
where agents are
built and hosted.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.030
You define agents with
models, tools, instructions,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.780
and you ground the responses in
knowledge and enterprise data.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.160
You evaluate, version,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:14.970
and observe your agent's
behavior in Microsoft Foundry.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.610
Foundry is where
your agent lives.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:19.500
On the right, we have
Azure Logic Apps.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:23.100
This becomes your automation
and orchestration platform.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:26.550
This is where agents are
invoked and orchestrated.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:30.960
You trigger agents from any
event or schedule using hundreds

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:34.620
of connectors that Logic
Apps offers natively.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.240
We bring improved experience

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:39.870
to smoothly use these
services together to automate

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:43.050
and operationalize your
AI agents and run them

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:44.880
in production at scale.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.760
So let's take a look at this
demo where we build agents

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.480
in Microsoft Foundry, and we
orchestrate and invoke them

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.610
